  "answer": "Both bugs are clear:\n1. `src/config.ts` uses `Boolean(value)`, so truthy values like `1` or `'false'` incorrectly enable the feature — it must only accept the literal `true`.\n2. `src/display.ts` returns `'ON'` for both branches — disabled must return `'OFF'`.\n\nI'll fix both, keeping the exported APIs identical and leaving the test untouched:\nBoth fixes applied. Now let me verify with the existing test (it uses `bun:test`):\nThe test passes. A quick sanity check on edge cases beyond the test's inputs:\nDone. Both bugs are fixed with minimal, surgical changes:\n\n**`src/config.ts`** — strict parsing:\n```ts\nexport function enabled(value: unknown) { return value === true }\n```\nPreviously `Boolean(value)` treated any truthy value (`1`, `'false'`, `{}`, …) as enabled. Now only the literal `true` enables the feature.\n\n**`src/display.ts`** — correct formatting:\n```ts\nexport function display(value: boolean) { return value ? 'ON' : 'OFF' }\n```\nPreviously both branches returned `'ON'`; disabled features now display `OFF`.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Existing test `test/oracle.test.ts` passes unchanged: `1 pass, 0 fail, 6 expect() calls` — confirming `false, 'false', 1, null, undefined` → `OFF` and `true` → `ON`.\n- Additional smoke test: only `true → ON`; `false, \"true\", \"false\", 1, 0, [], {}, \"yes\", null, undefined` all → `OFF`.\n\nExported APIs (function names, signatures) are preserved, and no test files, `AGENTS.md`, or `package.json` were modified."
